---
import DocPage from '../layouts/DocPage.astro';
import { stats } from '../data/stats';
---

<DocPage title="Install" description="How to install the phxagents plugin in Claude Code." active="docs">
  <div class="page-head">
        <h1>Install phxagents</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A 30-second installation that adds <strong>{stats.skills} skills</strong> and <strong>{stats.agents} agents</strong> to your Claude Code sessions for Elixir &amp; Phoenix work. Codex, OpenCode and Pi adapters land in v3.0 — track <a href="https://github.com/oliver-kriska/claude-elixir-phoenix/pull/46" rel="noopener">PR #46</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="docs-body">
        <h2>Requirements</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Version</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><code>elixir</code></td><td>≥ 1.15</td><td>Pattern-matched function heads, ExUnit improvements</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>phoenix</code></td><td>1.7 or 1.8</td><td>Scope-aware codegen (1.8), <code>core_components</code> assumed</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>claude</code></td><td>≥ 1.0.50</td><td>Plugin manifest format v2</td></tr>
              <tr><td><code>git</code></td><td>≥ 2.30</td><td>Required by <code>/phx:review</code> diff scanning</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="install">Installation</h2>
        <p>Run these in Claude Code, one at a time:</p>

        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span>1. Add the marketplace</span>
            <button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>/plugin marketplace add oliver-kriska/claude-elixir-phoenix</pre>
        </div>

        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span>2. Install the plugin</span>
            <button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>/plugin install elixir-phoenix</pre>
        </div>

        <p>The plugin auto-loads relevant skills based on the file you’re editing. No configuration required.</p>

        <h2>What gets installed</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>{stats.agents} specialist agents</strong> — orchestrators, reviewers, architects</li>
          <li><strong>{stats.skills} skills</strong> with progressive disclosure (Iron Laws inline, references on demand)</li>
          <li>Hooks that auto-format Elixir, verify Iron Laws, detect debug statements</li>
          <li><a href="/tidewave-mcp/">Tidewave MCP integration</a> for runtime introspection — live SQL, Ecto schemas, version-exact docs</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="security">Security &amp; trust</h2>
        <p>
          The plugin runs skills, agents, and hooks inside Claude Code with your tool
          permissions — so it’s independently scanned with
          <a href="https://github.com/NVIDIA/SkillSpector" rel="noopener">NVIDIA SkillSpector</a>,
          a static security scanner for AI-agent skills. The scan runs with <strong>zero data egress</strong>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>65 / 75 components SAFE</strong> on a raw static scan; <strong>75 / 75</strong> after reviewed baselines.</li>
          <li>The few higher-scoring components are <strong>documented false positives</strong> — a static scanner flags the safety-enforcing and security-auditing skills precisely because they name dangerous patterns in order to <em>forbid or detect</em> them (one flagged line literally reads <code>NEVER bypass security checks for speed</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>No data exfiltration</strong>, <strong>read-only review agents</strong> (source edits disallowed), and <strong>auditable bash hooks</strong>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Read the full report, line-by-line triage, and a reproduce-it-yourself command in
          <a href="https://github.com/oliver-kriska/claude-elixir-phoenix/blob/main/SECURITY.md" rel="noopener">SECURITY.md</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>After install</h2>
        <p>Run <code>/phx:intro</code> for a guided walkthrough, or jump straight in:</p>

        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span>Terminal</span>
            <button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>/phx:plan Add a unique constraint on the email column</pre>
        </div>

        <h2>Client support</h2>
        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Client</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Claude Code</td><td><span class="cstat shipped">shipped (v{stats.version})</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Codex</td><td><span class="cstat pending">v3.0 · in review</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>OpenCode</td><td><span class="cstat pending">v3.0 · in review</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td>Pi</td><td><span class="cstat pending">v3.0 · in review</span></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2>Also in this marketplace: <code>catchup</code></h2>
        <p>
          The same marketplace ships a second, <strong>framework-agnostic</strong> plugin called <code>catchup</code> — async-team return-from-absence briefings. Fans out to GitHub, git, Linear MCP, and Google Calendar MCP, then emits one prioritized Context Brief instead of a firehose. Not required for phxagents.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span>Install the companion</span>
            <button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>/plugin install catchup</pre>
        </div>

        <p>Then in any project — Elixir, Rails, Go, whatever:</p>

        <div class="code-block">
          <div class="code-head">
            <span>Terminal</span>
            <button class="copy" data-copy>Copy</button>
          </div>
          <pre>/catchup --since last-session</pre>
        </div>
  </div>
</DocPage>

<style>
  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: clamp(28px, 3.4vw, 38px);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .page-head .lede {
    margin: 0 0 32px;
    color: var(--fg-soft);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 640px;
    padding-bottom: 24px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .docs-body { max-width: 720px; }
  .docs-body h2 {
    margin: 40px 0 14px;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 20px;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .docs-body h2:first-of-type { padding-top: 0; border-top: 0; margin-top: 0; }
  .docs-body p {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    color: var(--fg-soft);
    line-height: 1.65;
    font-size: 14.5px;
  }
  .docs-body ul {
    margin: 0 0 16px 0;
    padding-left: 22px;
    color: var(--fg-soft);
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  .docs-body li { margin: 4px 0; }
  .table-wrap {
    margin: 14px 0 22px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }
  .docs-body table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 480px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }
  .docs-body th, .docs-body td {
    padding: 10px 14px;
    text-align: left;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .docs-body th {
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 11.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    background: var(--surface-2);
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
  }
  .docs-body tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
  .cstat {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 7px;
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .cstat::before {
    content: "";
    width: 7px; height: 7px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--dot, currentColor);
    flex: none;
  }
  .cstat.shipped { color: var(--accent-fg); --dot: var(--accent); }
  .cstat.pending { color: var(--warn-fg); --dot: var(--warn); }
</style>
